#: ../../../faq.rst:2
msgid "FAQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:4
msgid "This section answers common questions and pitfalls when using psutil."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:7
msgid "General"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:12
msgid "Why should I avoid positional unpacking of named tuples?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:14
msgid ""
"Most psutil functions return named tuples. It is tempting to unpack them "
"positionally, but **field order may change across major releases** (as "
"happened in 8.0 with :func:`cpu_times` and :meth:`Process.memory_info`). "
"Always use attribute access instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:19
msgid ""
"# bad\n"
"rss, vms = p.memory_info()\n"
"\n"
"# good\n"
"m = p.memory_info()\n"
"print(m.rss, m.vms)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:28
msgid ""
"See the :ref:`migration guide <migration-8.0>` for the full list of field-"
"order changes in 8.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:32
msgid "Exceptions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:37
msgid "Why do I get AccessDenied?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:39
msgid ""
":exc:`AccessDenied` is raised when the OS refuses to return information "
"about a process because the calling user does not have sufficient "
"privileges. This is expected behavior and is not a bug. It typically happens "
"when:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:43
msgid "querying processes owned by other users (e.g. *root*)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:44
msgid ""
"calling certain methods like :meth:`Process.memory_maps`, :meth:`Process."
"open_files` or :meth:`Process.net_connections` for privileged processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:48
msgid "You have two options to deal with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:50
msgid "Option 1: call the method directly and catch the exception:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:52
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"p = psutil.Process(pid)\n"
"try:\n"
"    print(p.memory_maps())\n"
"except (psutil.AccessDenied, psutil.NoSuchProcess):\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:62
msgid ""
"Option 2: use :func:`process_iter` with a list of attribute names to pre-"
"fetch. Both :exc:`AccessDenied` and :exc:`NoSuchProcess` are handled "
"internally: the corresponding method returns ``None`` (or ``ad_value``) "
"instead of raising. This also avoids the race condition where a process "
"disappears between iteration and method call:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:68
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter([\"name\", \"username\"], ad_value=\"N/A\"):\n"
"    print(p.name(), p.username())  # no try/except needed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:78
msgid "Why do I get NoSuchProcess?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:80
msgid ""
":exc:`NoSuchProcess` is raised when a process no longer exists. The most "
"common cause is a race condition: a process can die between the moment its "
"PID is obtained and the moment it is queried. The following two naive "
"patterns are racy:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:85
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for pid in psutil.pids():\n"
"    p = psutil.Process(pid)  # may raise NoSuchProcess\n"
"    print(p.name())  # may raise NoSuchProcess"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:93
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"if psutil.pid_exists(pid):\n"
"    p = psutil.Process(pid)  # may raise NoSuchProcess\n"
"    print(p.name())  # may raise NoSuchProcess"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:101
msgid ""
"The correct approach is to use :func:`process_iter`, which handles :exc:"
"`NoSuchProcess` internally and skips processes that disappear during "
"iteration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:105
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter([\"name\"]):\n"
"    print(p.name())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:112
msgid ""
"If you have a specific PID (e.g. a known child process), wrap the call in a "
"try/except:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:115
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"try:\n"
"    p = psutil.Process(pid)\n"
"    print(p.name(), p.status())\n"
"except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:125
msgid ""
"You can also catch :exc:`Error`, which implies both :exc:`AccessDenied` and :"
"exc:`NoSuchProcess`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:128
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"try:\n"
"    p = psutil.Process(pid)\n"
"    print(p.name(), p.status())\n"
"except psutil.Error:\n"
"    pass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:139
msgid "Processes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:144
msgid "PID reuse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:146
msgid ""
"Operating systems recycle PIDs. A :class:`Process` object obtained now may "
"later refer to a different process if the original one terminated and a new "
"one was assigned the same PID."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:150
msgid "**How psutil handles this:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:152
msgid ""
"*Most read-only methods* (e.g. :meth:`Process.name`, :meth:`Process."
"cpu_percent`) do **not** check for PID reuse and instead query whatever "
"process currently holds that PID."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:156
msgid ""
"*Signal methods* (e.g. :meth:`Process.send_signal`, :meth:`Process."
"suspend`, :meth:`Process.resume`, :meth:`Process.terminate`, :meth:`Process."
"kill`) **do** check for PID reuse (via PID + creation time) before acting, "
"raising :exc:`NoSuchProcess` if the PID was recycled. This prevents "
"accidentally killing the wrong process (:bpo:`6973`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:162
msgid ""
"*Set methods* :meth:`Process.nice` (set), :meth:`Process.ionice` (set), :"
"meth:`Process.cpu_affinity` (set), and :meth:`Process.rlimit` (set) also "
"perform this check before applying changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:166
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.is_running` is the recommended way to verify whether a :class:"
"`Process` instance still refers to the same process. It compares PID and "
"creation time, and returns ``False`` if the PID was reused. Prefer it over :"
"func:`pid_exists`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:173
msgid ""
"On FreeBSD, OpenBSD, SunOS and AIX the PID reuse check is disabled, and "
"process identity is based on the PID alone. That's because on these "
"platforms the process creation time is not stable across system clock "
"updates (e.g. NTP), which previously caused false :exc:`NoSuchProcess` "
"exceptions for processes which were still alive (:gh:`2888`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:182
msgid "What is a zombie process?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:184
msgid ""
"A :term:`zombie process` is a process that has finished execution but whose "
"entry remains in the process table until the parent calls ``wait()``. When "
"psutil encounters a :term:`zombie process` it raises :exc:`ZombieProcess`, a "
"subclass of :exc:`NoSuchProcess`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:189
msgid "**Behavior:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:191
msgid ""
"A zombie process can be instantiated via :class:`Process` (pid) without "
"error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:193
msgid ":meth:`Process.status` always returns :data:`STATUS_ZOMBIE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:194
msgid ":meth:`Process.is_running` and :func:`pid_exists` return ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:195
msgid "The zombie appears in :func:`process_iter` and :func:`pids`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:196
msgid ""
"Sending signals (:meth:`Process.terminate`, :meth:`Process.kill`, etc.) has "
"no effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:198
msgid ""
"Most methods (:meth:`Process.cmdline`, :meth:`Process.exe`, :meth:`Process."
"memory_maps`, etc.) may raise :exc:`ZombieProcess`, return a meaningful "
"value, or return a null/empty value depending on the platform."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:201
msgid ":meth:`Process.as_dict` will not crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:203
msgid "**How to create a zombie:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:205
msgid ""
"import os, time\n"
"\n"
"pid = os.fork()  # the zombie\n"
"if pid == 0:\n"
"    os._exit(0)  # child exits immediately\n"
"else:\n"
"    time.sleep(1000)  # parent does NOT call wait()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:215
msgid "**How to detect zombies:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:217
msgid ""
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"for p in psutil.process_iter([\"status\"]):\n"
"    if p.status() == psutil.STATUS_ZOMBIE:\n"
"        print(f\"zombie: pid={p.pid}\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:225
msgid "**How to get rid of a zombie:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:227
msgid ""
"The only way is to have its parent process call ``wait()`` (or "
"``waitpid()``). If the parent never does this, killing the parent will cause "
"the zombie to be re-parented to ``init`` / ``systemd``, which will reap it "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:234
msgid "Why does open_files() not return all files on Windows?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:236
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.open_files` on Windows is not guaranteed to enumerate all "
"regular file handles. The underlying Windows API may hang when retrieving "
"certain :term:`handle` names, so psutil spawns a thread to query each handle "
"and kills it if it doesn't respond within 100 ms. This means some entries "
"can be missed. This is a known OS-level limitation shared by tools like "
"Process Hacker (see `issue 597 <https://github.com/giampaolo/psutil/"
"pull/597>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:246
msgid "What is the difference between pid_exists() and Process.is_running()?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:248
msgid ""
":func:`pid_exists` checks whether a PID is present in the process list. :"
"meth:`Process.is_running` does the same, but also detects :ref:`PID reuse "
"<faq_pid_reuse>` by comparing the process creation time. Use :func:"
"`pid_exists` when you have a bare PID and don't need to guard against reuse "
"(it's faster). Use :meth:`Process.is_running` when you hold a :class:"
"`Process` object and want to confirm it still refers to the same process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:257
msgid "CPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:262
msgid "Why does cpu_percent() return 0.0 on first call?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:264
msgid ""
":func:`cpu_percent` (and :meth:`Process.cpu_percent`) measures CPU usage "
"*between two calls*. The very first call has no prior sample to compare "
"against, so it always returns ``0.0``. The fix is to call it once to "
"initialize the baseline, discard the result, then call it again after a "
"short sleep:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:269
msgid ""
"import time\n"
"import psutil\n"
"\n"
"psutil.cpu_percent()          # discard first call\n"
"time.sleep(0.5)\n"
"print(psutil.cpu_percent())   # meaningful value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:278
msgid "Alternatively, pass ``interval`` to make it block internally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:280
msgid "print(psutil.cpu_percent(interval=0.5))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:284
msgid "The same applies to :meth:`Process.cpu_percent`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:286
msgid ""
"p = psutil.Process()\n"
"p.cpu_percent()               # discard\n"
"time.sleep(0.5)\n"
"print(p.cpu_percent())        # meaningful value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:296
msgid "Can Process.cpu_percent() return a value higher than 100%?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:298
msgid ""
"Yes. On a multi-core system a process can run threads on several CPUs at the "
"same time. The maximum value is ``psutil.cpu_count() * 100``. For example, "
"on a 4-core machine a fully-loaded process can reach 400%. The system-wide :"
"func:`cpu_percent` (without a :class:`Process`) always stays in the 0–100% "
"range because it averages across all cores."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:304
msgid ""
"The returned value is explicitly *not* split evenly between all available "
"CPUs. This is consistent with the ``top`` UNIX utility: a busy loop on a "
"system with 2 :term:`logical CPUs <logical CPU>` is reported as 100%, not "
"50%. Note that Windows ``taskmgr.exe`` behaves differently (it would report "
"50%). To emulate that: ``p.cpu_percent() / psutil.cpu_count()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:313
msgid ""
"What is the difference between psutil, os, and multiprocessing cpu_count()?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:315
msgid ""
":func:`os.cpu_count` returns the number of :term:`logical CPUs <logical "
"CPU>` (including hyperthreads). It is the same as ``psutil."
"cpu_count(logical=True)``, but psutil does not honour :envvar:"
"`PYTHON_CPU_COUNT` environment variable introduced in Python 3.13."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:319
msgid ""
":func:`os.process_cpu_count` (Python 3.13+) returns the number of CPUs the "
"calling process is **allowed to use** (respects :term:`CPU affinity` and "
"cgroups). The psutil equivalent is ``len(psutil.Process().cpu_affinity())``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:322
msgid ""
":func:`multiprocessing.cpu_count` returns the same value as :func:`os."
"process_cpu_count` (Python 3.13+)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:324
msgid ""
":func:`psutil.cpu_count` with ``logical=False`` returns the number of :term:"
"`physical cores <physical CPU>`, which has no stdlib equivalent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:328
msgid "Memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:333
msgid "What is the difference between virtual_memory() available and free?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:335
msgid ""
":func:`virtual_memory` returns both :field:`free` and :field:`available`, "
"but they measure different things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:338
msgid ":field:`free`: memory that is not being used at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:339
msgid ""
":field:`available`: how much memory can be given to processes without :term:"
"`swapping <swap memory>`. This includes reclaimable :term:`caches <page "
"cache>` and :term:`buffers` that the OS can reclaim under pressure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:344
msgid ""
"In practice, :field:`available` is almost always the metric you want when "
"monitoring memory. :field:`free` can be misleadingly low on systems where "
"the OS aggressively uses RAM for caches (which is normal and healthy). On "
"Windows, :field:`free` and :field:`available` are the same value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:352
msgid "What is the difference between RSS and VMS?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:354
msgid ""
":field:`rss` (:term:`Resident Set Size <RSS>`) is the amount of physical "
"memory (RAM) currently mapped into the process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:356
msgid ""
":field:`vms` (:term:`Virtual Memory Size <VMS>`) is the total virtual "
"address space of the process, including memory that has been :term:`swapped "
"out <swap-out>`, shared libraries, and :term:`memory-mapped files <mapped "
"memory>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:361
msgid ""
":field:`rss` is generally the most useful metric for answering \"how much "
"RAM is this process using?\". Note that it includes :term:`shared memory`, "
"so it may overestimate actual usage when compared across processes. :field:"
"`vms` is generally larger and can be misleadingly high, as it includes "
"memory that is not resident in physical RAM. Both values are portable across "
"platforms and are returned by :meth:`Process.memory_info`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:371
msgid "When should I use memory_footprint() vs memory_info()?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:373
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.memory_info` returns :field:`rss` (:term:`Resident Set Size "
"<RSS>`), which includes :term:`shared libraries <shared memory>` counted in "
"every process that uses them. For example, if ``libc`` uses 2 MB and 100 "
"processes map it, each process includes those 2 MB in its :field:`rss`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:379
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.memory_footprint` returns :field:`uss` (:term:`Unique Set "
"Size <USS>`), i.e. :term:`private memory` of the process. It represents the "
"amount of memory that would be freed if the process were terminated right "
"now. It is more accurate than :term:`RSS`, but substantially slower and "
"requires higher privileges. On Linux it also returns :field:`pss` (:term:"
"`Proportional Set Size <PSS>`) and :term:`swap <swap memory>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:389
msgid "Why does virtual_memory() used + free != total?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:391
msgid ""
"Because some memory (like :term:`page cache` and :term:`buffers`) is "
"reclaimable and accounted separately:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:394
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> m = psutil.virtual_memory()\n"
">>> m.used + m.free == m.total\n"
"False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../faq.rst:401
msgid ""
"The :field:`available` field already includes this reclaimable memory and is "
"the best indicator of memory pressure. See :ref:"
"`faq_virtual_memory_available`."
msgstr ""
